            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of microlaparoscopy and conventional laparoscopy for tubal sterilization under local anesthesia with mild sedation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>385-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare tubal sterilization performed by microlaparoscopy and conventional laparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, randomized trial (Canadian Task Force classification I).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Gazi University School of Medicine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty women undergoing surgical sterilization.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ten sterilizations by conventional laparoscopy and 10 by microlaparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The techniques were comparable in quality of visualization, operating time, amount of drugs used for sedation and local anesthesia, and intraoperative pain scores. However, the postoperative analgesic requirement was significantly less in women treated by by microlaparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Tubal sterilization by microlaparoscopy does not differ greatly from conventional laparoscopic sterilization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
